<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure_level" target="energy_supply_shock::energy_supply_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.85</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating sanctions that target energy exports, shipping services, insurance and finance directly block or complicate shipments and sales from affected producers, reducing net volumetric flows of crude and gas to global markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">energy_supply_shock</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure_level" target="global_bond_turmoil::cross_border_flows" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Broad, enforced sanctions (including SWIFT exclusions and secondary sanctions) and associated correspondent‑bank de‑risking directly block or reroute payments and reduce banking intermediations, causing an immediate fall in gross cross‑border capital and payment flows through affected corridors.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_turmoil</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure_level" target="global_bond_turmoil::long_term_bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Targeted sanctions that restrict bank access, commodity exports, or settlement channels increase counterparty and liquidity risk in fixed‑income markets, widening term premia and pushing up long‑term sovereign yields as investors demand compensation for higher tail and liquidity risk.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">global_bond_turmoil</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure_level" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::industrial_policy_streng" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Widening and intensifying sanctions (including secondary controls) prompt governments to expand export controls, domestic subsidies and procurement rules to secure AI-relevant supply chains and onshore production, strengthening industrial-policy interventions aimed at AI and semiconductors.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::sanctions_pressure_level" target="us_iran_confrontation::military_deployment_leve" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating sanctions on Russia drive Moscow into deeper military-technical and intelligence cooperation with Tehran, which in turn prompts the U.S. to increase regional deployments to deter or counter enhanced Iran-linked capabilities.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_confrontation</data>
</edge>
